 figs:{chinese:["Celestial Ford"],japanese:["Celestial Ford"],potawatomi:["Crane / Swan / Human skeleton-like creature with wings"],belarusian:["The Cross"],hawaiian_starlines:["Navigator's Triangle"],arabic_hand:["The Riders","The One Riding Behind"],ojibwe_hand:["The Crane"]},
 tales:{
  greek:{title:"The Swan on the River of Stars",status:"well-documented · two rival stories",text:
"The Greeks agreed this was a swan flying down the Milky Way, wings spread, long neck stretched south. They did not agree whose swan.\n\nIn one story it is Zeus himself, in the shape he took to come to Leda, queen of Sparta. From that meeting came Helen, whose face launched the Trojan War, and the twins Castor and Pollux.\n\nIn the other it is a grieving friend. When Phaethon lost control of the Sun's chariot and Zeus struck him down, he fell burning into the river Eridanus. His companion Cycnus dived again and again to gather the body for burial, until the gods, pitying him, turned him into a swan and set him in the sky. That, the story says, is why swans still dip their heads under the water."},
  arabic:{title:"The Hen and the Riders",status:"well-documented",text:
"The star at the top of the cross tells you what Arab astronomers saw: Deneb is from dhanab al-dajāja, 'the tail of the hen.' Following Ptolemy, they drew a great bird here, and the name of its tail has outlived the bird.\n\nThe older Arabian picture was different. The bright stars across the swan's wings were al-Fawāris, the Riders — horsemen crossing the Milky Way in a line — and Deneb, coming along after them, was al-Ridf: the one who rides behind."},
  chinese:{title:"Tiān Jīn — the Celestial Ford",status:"well-documented",text:
"To Chinese eyes the Milky Way was the Silver River, Yín Hé, flowing across the sky. A river needs a crossing, and this is it: Tiān Jīn, the Celestial Ford, nine stars spanning the stream at its brightest point, with Deneb among them. Officials and travellers of the heavenly court cross here.\n\nThe ford lies between the two most famous stars on the river's banks — the Weaving Girl (Vega) on one side and the Cowherd (Altair) on the other — and their story is the best-known tale in the Chinese sky. Tap Lyra to hear it."},
  hungarian:{title:"Hadak Útja — the Road of the Warriors",status:"well-documented",text:
"The band of light that runs through the Swan has a Hungarian name older than any telescope: Hadak Útja, the Road of the Warriors.\n\nPrince Csaba was the youngest son of Attila, king of the Huns. After his father's death the empire broke apart, and Csaba led what remained of his people east, leaving behind the Székelys to hold the land in Transylvania, with a promise: if they were ever in mortal danger, he would come back. Generations passed, and enemies gathered against the Székelys until they stood at the edge of defeat. Then the night sky opened. Down a shining road rode Csaba at the head of his army of the dead, and the invaders fled.\n\nThe road never faded. The Milky Way is the track his horsemen made, and the stars in it are the sparks struck from their horseshoes."},
  hawaiian_starlines:{title:"The Navigator's Triangle",status:"documented · a modern navigators' teaching",text:
"Hawaiian voyagers today learn the sky as four great 'star lines,' a system developed by navigator Nainoa Thompson from the teaching of Mau Piailug of Satawal and used on the voyaging canoe Hōkūleʻa. In it, Deneb, Vega and Altair are Hawaiki, Keoe and Humu — the Navigator's Triangle.\n\nThe three stars stand for the three corners of the Polynesian world: Hawaiʻi, Rapa Nui and Aotearoa. Below them in the southern sky hangs the fish-hook of the demigod Māui — Scorpius — which is said to have pulled those islands up from the sea. Deneb, with the star at the tip of the swan's wing, makes a pointer toward north."},
  more:{title:"Other skies, other names",status:"names from published sky-culture data",text:
"• Christian Europe: the Northern Cross. Belarusian: Petrou Krest — Peter's Cross. Romanian: Crucea mare — the Great Cross.\n• Potawatomi: Thethak, the Crane — also read as a swan, or as a winged skeleton-like being.\n• Macedonian: Svrdel — the Auger.\n• Tahiti and the Society Islands: Deneb is Piraʻe-tea, the white sea-swallow."}
 }
