 tales:{
  greek:{title:"The Lyre of Orpheus",status:"well-documented",text:
"The first lyre was made by Hermes on the day he was born, from the shell of a tortoise. He gave it to Apollo, and Apollo gave it to Orpheus, the greatest musician who ever lived. When Orpheus played, wild animals lay down to listen, trees leaned toward him and rivers stopped.\n\nWhen his wife Eurydice died of a snakebite, he went down alive into the underworld and played for Hades and Persephone until even they wept. They let her go on one condition: he must walk ahead and not look back until both stood in the sunlight. At the very threshold he turned, and she was gone for good.\n\nOrpheus wandered, refusing all comfort, and was at last torn apart. His lyre drifted down the river, still sounding, and Zeus lifted it into the sky."},
  arabic:{title:"The Swooping Eagle",status:"well-documented",text:
"Vega is one of the oldest Arabic star names used in Europe, and it is half of a phrase. The desert Arabs saw two eagles in this part of the sky. One has its wings spread wide: an-nasr aṭ-ṭā'ir, the Flying Eagle, which became Altair. The other has its wings folded back, dropping on its prey: an-nasr al-wāqiʿ, the Swooping (or Alighting) Eagle. The bright star is the bird's body, and the two small stars beside it make the folded wings.\n\nMedieval translators kept only the last word, wāqiʿ, and wrote it Wega — and so the fifth-brightest star in the sky is still called 'falling.'"},
  chinese:{title:"The Weaving Girl and the Cowherd",status:"well-documented",text:
"Vega is Zhī Nǚ, the Weaving Girl, a daughter of heaven who wove the coloured clouds. Across the Silver River — the Milky Way — stands Altair, Niú Láng, the Cowherd, a mortal who had nothing but an old ox.\n\nThey met, fell in love and married, and were so happy that she left her loom and the sky lost its colours. The Queen Mother of the West was furious. She took the Weaving Girl back to heaven, and when the Cowherd followed, carrying their two children in baskets on a pole across his shoulders, she drew her hairpin across the sky and scratched a river between them.\n\nBut their grief moved even heaven. Once a year, on the seventh night of the seventh month, all the magpies in the world fly up and make a bridge with their wings, and the two cross to each other for a single night. The festival is Qīxī; in Japan the same story is Tanabata."},
  boorong:{title:"Neilloan, the Malleefowl",status:"well-documented",text:
"To the Boorong people of Lake Tyrrell in north-western Victoria, Vega and Lyra are Neilloan, the ancestral malleefowl — the mother of all malleefowl, a ground bird that builds a huge mound of sand and leaf litter and lets the heat of the rotting leaves hatch her eggs.\n\nNeilloan is a calendar. From southern Australia Lyra is in the night sky only from about March to October, the same months the birds are scraping their mounds together. When she is seen for the last time, low after sunset in early October, the birds have begun to lay, and it is time to go looking for eggs. The Boorong said it was Neilloan herself who taught them when."},
  inuit:{title:"Kingulliq — the Old Woman Behind",status:"documented · brief",text:
"Vega in the Inuit sky is Kingulliq, 'the one behind.' She is a grandmother, and she is at the tail end of a chase that crosses half the sky. Ahead of her are Sivulliik, 'the two in front' — an orphan boy running for his life and the angry old man pursuing him, seen in the stars of Boötes. Kingulliq comes after them both. The three were lifted into the sky mid-pursuit and have followed one another across it ever since."},
  japanese:{title:"Tanabata — Orihime and Hikoboshi",status:"well-documented",text:
"In Japan the Chinese story of the Weaving Girl and the Cowherd became Tanabata, the Star Festival, kept on the seventh night of the seventh month.\n\nVega is Orihime, the Weaving Princess, daughter of the Sky King, who wove beautiful cloth on the bank of Amanogawa, the River of Heaven. Altair is Hikoboshi, the cowherd on the far shore. They married, and in their happiness she stopped weaving and his cattle wandered across the sky. The Sky King put the river between them and allowed them one meeting a year. If it rains on Tanabata the river floods and they must wait another year — so everyone hopes for a clear night.\n\nPeople write wishes on strips of coloured paper and tie them to bamboo branches. Sendai holds its great Tanabata in August, on the old calendar's date."},
  more:{title:"Other skies, other names",status:"names from published sky-culture data",text:
"• Hindu: Abhijit, 'the Victorious' — a 28th lunar mansion counted outside the usual 27.\n• Sámi: Vega is the Runner, one of the hunters closing in on the great elk Sarva.\n• Māori: Whānui. Hawaiian navigators: Keoe, a corner of the Navigator's Triangle.\n• Belarusian: Niavesta — the Bride. Romanian: the Shepherd with His Sheep.\n• Babylonian: the She-Goat."}
 }
