 figs:{chinese:["Drum at the River"],japanese:["Drum at the River"],inuit:["Two Sunbeams"],boorong:["Purple crowned lorikeet"],indian:["Ear"],arabic_hand:["The Flying Vulture"]},
 tales:{
  greek:{title:"The Eagle of Zeus",status:"well-documented",text:
"The eagle was Zeus's own bird — the only creature, it was said, that could look straight at the sun — and it carried his thunderbolts in its claws during the war against the Titans.\n\nIts most famous errand was a kidnapping. Ganymede, a prince of Troy, was the most beautiful of mortal boys. Zeus sent the eagle (or became it) to snatch him from the slopes of Mount Ida and bear him up to Olympus, where he was made cupbearer to the gods and given eternal youth. Look below and to the east of the Eagle and you find him still pouring: he is Aquarius, the Water-Carrier."},
  arabic:{title:"The Flying Eagle",status:"well-documented",text:
"Altair's name is the second half of a pair. The desert Arabs saw two eagles on either side of the Milky Way. Vega, with its two small companions tucked in close, was the eagle with folded wings, dropping to earth. Altair is the other one: an-nasr aṭ-ṭā'ir, the Flying Eagle, with a star on either side — Tarazed and Alshain — for wings stretched out in level flight.\n\nEuropean translators shortened the phrase to its last word, aṭ-ṭā'ir, 'the flyer.' This is a rare case where the Greek and Arabian pictures agree: both saw an eagle here."},
  chinese:{title:"The Cowherd and the Drum at the River",status:"well-documented",text:
"Official Chinese star maps call Altair and its two flanking stars Hé Gǔ, the Drum at the River — a great war drum set up on the bank of the Silver River, the Milky Way.\n\nBut everyone knows these three by another story. Altair is Niú Láng, the Cowherd, and the two fainter stars beside him are his children, carried in baskets hung from a pole across his shoulders as he chases after his wife, the Weaving Girl — Vega, on the far bank. The river of stars was scratched between them, and they may cross it only once a year, when the magpies make their bridge. The full story is under Lyra."},
  inuit:{title:"Aagjuuk — heralds of the returning sun",status:"well-documented",text:
"North of the Arctic Circle the sun disappears in early winter and does not rise for weeks. In that dark the most important stars in the Inuit sky are a pair: Aagjuuk, which are Altair and its neighbour Tarazed.\n\nAround the middle of December they show for the first time in the dawn twilight, low in the north-east — and that first sighting, right at the winter solstice, was the signal across the Arctic that the dark had reached its deepest and the light would now begin to come back. In many places it was the cue for the midwinter gathering. Aagjuuk also served as a clock, telling how far the long night had run. In Greenland the same two stars are Aassuutit, and it is said that they stopped the sun from sinking away for ever."},
  more:{title:"Other skies, other names",status:"names from published sky-culture data",text:
"• Boorong (Victoria, Australia): Altair is Totyarguil, the purple-crowned lorikeet.\n• Hindu: Śravaṇa, 'the Ear' or 'Hearing' — a lunar mansion.\n• Māori: Poutū-te-rangi. Hawaiian navigators: Humu, a corner of the Navigator's Triangle.\n• Romanian: the Emperor's Daughter with a Yoke.\n• Babylonian: the Eagle — the same bird, two thousand years before the Greeks."}
 }
